{"qid":"Q796583","names":["Kingdom of Kongo"],"title":"Kingdom of Kongo","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Kongo was a Central African polity straddling the lower Congo River, stretching from the Atlantic coast to the Kwango River and encompassing parts of present-day Angola, Democratic Republic of the Congo, and Republic of the Congo. Founded in the late fourteenth century, it reached its apex under Afonso I (r. 1509–1542), who embraced Christianity, conducted direct diplomacy with Portugal, and built one of the most sophisticated administrative systems in sub-Saharan Africa. The Battle of Mbwila (1665), in which Portuguese-allied forces defeated and killed Manikongo António I, shattered central authority; the kingdom fragmented into rival factions and never reunified as a single state.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. General territorial boundaries are reconstructed from Portuguese missionary reports and administrative records; inner provincial boundaries remain coarsely attested.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Kingdom of Kongo","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kongo-kingdom"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Kongo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Kongo"},{"label":"Met Heilbrunn: Kingdom of Kongo","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/kong/hd_kong.htm"}],"britannica":"place/Kongo-historical-kingdom-Africa","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Kongo","rulers":[{"qid":"Q1522957","name":"João I of Kongo","start":1470,"end":1506,"image":"Jean Roy de Congo.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jean%20Roy%20de%20Congo.jpg?widt